| `+` on two objects merges them, the way two arrays concatenate. Arrays
| cannot have duplicate keys and objects can, so a merge has one extra rule:
| a field named by both sides takes the *right* value and keeps the *left*
| position, which makes `a + b` read as "a, with b's fields applied".
assert { a = 1 } + { b = 2 } = { a = 1, b = 2 }
assert { a = 1, b = 2 } + { a = 9 } = { a = 9, b = 2 }
assert {} + { a = 1 } = { a = 1 }
assert { a = 1 } + {} = { a = 1 }
assert {} + {} = {}

| Position is real but is not part of an object's identity: equality goes by
| field name, so overriding in place and dropping-and-re-appending are the
| same value. Iteration is what sees the difference.
assert { a = 1, b = 2 } + { a = 9 } = { b = 2, a = 9 }
order = []
loop k, v over { a = 1, b = 2 } + { a = 9 }
    order += k
assert order = ["a", "b"]

| Fields are replaced whole; nothing merges recursively.
assert { n = { x = 1 } } + { n = { y = 2 } } = { n = { y = 2 } }
assert { xs = [1] } + { xs = [2] } = { xs = [2] }

| Neither operand is disturbed: `+` builds a new value, like every other
| operator in the language.
left = { a = 1 }
right = { a = 2 }
merged = left + right
assert left = { a = 1 }
assert right = { a = 2 }
assert merged = { a = 2 }

| The everyday idiom this exists for: copy a particle, change a field.
original = Reply { text = "hi", recipient = "ada" }
edited = original + { text = "bye" }
assert edited.text = "bye"
assert edited.recipient = "ada"
assert edited._class = "Reply"
assert edited ∈ Reply

| `_class` is an ordinary field, so a merge can re-tag a particle too. The
| particle sugar puts `_class` first, and the override keeps that position.
retagged = original + Draft {}
assert retagged ∈ Draft
assert retagged.text = "hi"

| `+=` is `x = x + x`'s sugar, so it inherits all of the above.
acc = {}
loop k, v over { a = 1, b = 2 }
    acc += { seen = k }
assert acc = { seen = "b" }

| Self-merge is a value operation, not an aliasing one.
same = { a = 1 }
same += same
assert same = { a = 1 }

| An array operand still wins: with one array involved, the object is a
| single *element*, not something to merge into. Unchanged behaviour.
assert [1] + { k = 2 } = [1, { k = 2 }]
assert { k = 2 } + [1] = [{ k = 2 }, 1]
